    "verification_method": "Per-batch label listings from GitHub AFTER creation (batch:A=9, batch:B=2, batch:C=4, batch:D=3, batch:E=6, batch:F=3, batch:G=9, batch:H=2; sum 38). Each listing returned fewer rows than the observed page cap, so each is complete. Counts are from GitHub listings, never from counting successful tool calls.",
    "page_cap_finding": "github_list_issues returns at most 25 rows. A state=all listing filtered by the v2.0.0 label returned exactly 25 (issues 269-293) while 38 carry that label. A caller trusting that single listing would have concluded 25 issues exist. Per-batch listings were used instead precisely because each stays under the cap.",
    "duplicate_summary": "Exactly one duplicate pair, from the first create call before the root cause was known. No duplicate occurred in the 36 subsequent creates, each of which was verified immediately after creation by a narrow label listing.",
    "duplicate_root_cause": "mcp-github-api/base/clients.py:338 returned Github(token) with PyGithub defaults. PyGithub GithubRetry adds POST to urllib3 allowed_methods; urllib3 omits POST by design because retrying a non-idempotent write duplicates the resource. With timeout=15 and a status_forcelist covering 500-599 plus 403, a POST that GitHub accepts but answers slowly is retried and creates the record twice. Fixed in commit 33af037 on branch fix/non-idempotent-post-retry. THE FIX WAS NOT LIVE DURING THIS RUN -- it requires an MCP server restart that did not happen. Every write in this run was made under the unfixed client."
  },
